---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="NTD R&D Funding | Claire Nyquist"
    description="Interactive charts exploring how R&D investment in neglected tropical diseases compares to their global health burden."
>
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <!-- Hero Section -->
        <div class="text-center mb-8 md:mb-12">
            <h1
                class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 md:mb-6"
            >
                NTD R&D Funding
            </h1>
            <p
                class="text-lg sm:text-xl text-gray-600 max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed px-4"
            >
                Are neglected tropical diseases funded in proportion to their global health burden?
            </p>
            <p
                class="text-base sm:text-lg text-gray-600 max-w-4xl mx-auto leading-relaxed mt-4 px-4"
            >
                These charts compare total global R&D funding against disease burden
                for 15 neglected tropical diseases. The bar chart ranks diseases by
                how much R&D funding they receive per disability-adjusted life year
                (DALY), directly revealing which diseases are underfunded relative to
                their burden. The scatterplot below shows the relationship between
                total burden and total funding.
            </p>
            <p
                class="text-base sm:text-lg text-gray-600 max-w-4xl mx-auto leading-relaxed mt-4 px-4"
            >
                <strong class="text-gray-700">R&D funding data</strong> comes from
                <a href="https://gfinderdata.policycuresresearch.org/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">G-FINDER</a>,
                a project of Policy Cures Research that has tracked global funding for
                neglected disease R&D annually since 2007. G-FINDER surveys over 200
                funding organizations — governments, philanthropies, pharmaceutical
                companies, and multilateral agencies — to produce the most comprehensive
                picture of who funds what in neglected disease research. The figures shown
                here are from the 2022 funding year, covering product development for
                drugs, vaccines, diagnostics, and vector control across approximately
                30 neglected diseases.
            </p>
            <p
                class="text-base sm:text-lg text-gray-600 max-w-4xl mx-auto leading-relaxed mt-4 px-4"
            >
                <strong class="text-gray-700">Disease burden (DALYs)</strong> comes from the
                <a href="https://www.who.int/data/global-health-estimates" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO Global Health Estimates</a>
                (2019 revision). One DALY represents one lost year of healthy life — either
                through premature death or living with disability. DALYs capture the full
                health impact of a disease in a single number, making it possible to
                compare conditions as different as malaria (55.4 million DALYs) and
                leprosy (40,000 DALYs) on the same scale.
            </p>
            <p
                class="text-base sm:text-lg text-gray-600 max-w-4xl mx-auto leading-relaxed mt-4 px-4"
            >
                <strong class="text-gray-700">GDP per capita</strong> (used in the
                underlying dataset) is drawn from the
                <a href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NY.GDP.PCAP.CD" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Bank Open Data</a>
                platform (indicator NY.GDP.PCAP.CD, 2022 current USD). The dataset
                covers 32 funder countries, from low-income nations like Ethiopia and
                Uganda to high-income countries like Switzerland and Luxembourg.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Visualization placeholder -->
        <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border overflow-hidden p-8 text-center text-gray-400 min-h-[400px] flex items-center justify-center">
            <p class="text-lg">Visualization coming soon</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</BaseLayout>
